==Blog Update - Thursday, January 11th==
We're back with a brief update on some of the bits within this poll blog!

For starters, we're delaying the poll, which will now take place between Monday, January 15th and Friday, January 19th. Our reasoning for this is to give us a little breathing room for you to process the information in here, but to still make sure the poll wraps up before our Winter Summit, since we'll be following the Summit up with our usual Summit Greenlight Poll and need the Poll system clear. 

The main reason for today's blog update is that we're going to be shelving the Bladed Moon again. We'd done this once before and said we'd re-surface it after making our intentions for the Scythe of Vitur and Osmumten's Fang clear, which we did, and it's clearly still not hitting the mark for many of you. That's the long and short of it, but we'll go into a little more detail on why we've made this decision in case you'd like a bit of light reading!

First up - and perhaps the most widespread from the feedback we saw yesterday - is that the Bladed Moon just isn't resonating with many of you, including the people it's aimed at. While it seems many of you understand what the Moon is trying to achieve, it feels like the actual execution and numbers behind it aren't hitting the mark even in light of a newer context. Some of this concern stems from how well the Moon keeps pace with the Avernic Defender and that its power is disproportionally high for an item rewarded from content we've branded as 'around Barrows-tier'.

While we've seen some players suggest that the Avernic Defender could be improved to create a larger space between itself and the Dragon Defender (which could be filled by a weaker Bladed Moon), we feel this is a separate topic and slightly beyond the scope of what we're looking to achieve with Perilous Moons. This means many of you have concerns about weakening the appeal of the Theatre of Blood, since many of the rewards from Old School's second raid have been powercrept by newer offerings, and that offering a more obtainable alternative to the Avernic Defender might diminish the raid's appeal - which isn't something we'd like to do, especially with Perilous Moons' intent being to pipeline more people into learning about PvM and hopefully aspiring to become ToB enjoyers themselves!

On top of this, some feedback felt that pairing the Blade of Saeldor with the Bladed Moon resulted in a damage output that's equivalent-to or (in some cases) better-than the current combination of Osmumten's Fang and the Avernic Defender. This undermines some of our efforts to make the Scythe stand sufficiently clear of second-best options at some content and we believe it's a valid point that's been well-argued by many of you.

Those are perhaps the two most widespread sentiments we've seen in responses, but we've had players reach out to us about concerns that aren't as immediately obvious while still being impactful in ways we hadn't initially intended. The first of these relates to pairing the Bladed Moon with a Dragon Dagger and resulting in the iconic dagger becoming a more potent Special Attack option than the equally iconic Dragon Claws in a number of scenarios - given their difference in availability and cost, this is actually a pretty substantial point around an issue we hadn't anticipated, since we're not looking to shake up the Special Attack DPS meta with this off-hand offering.

The second of these relates to imbalancing PvP at some brackets, where builds with 65+ Defence (generally 70/75 Defence 'Med' builds) gain a huge increase in offensive potential against builds with lower Defence (typically 45 Defence 'Zerk' builds or 1 Defence 'Pure' builds). While we know this won't be a dealbreaker for some of you, we feel it's important to support builds like these since they tend to be easy-to-build while serving as solid entry-points for aspiring PvPers and would sooner improve the ability of these account types to punch up than see even more people trend towards these higher-level builds - it's a delicate balance that we're not looking to upset further at this point, and is a side-effect that we hadn't intended from our initial design.

Alongside all of the above, there was a sense of uncertainty about where the Bladed Moon might sit after you've got more information on Project Rebalance, which meant some of you felt uncomfortable with voting on such an impactful item without knowing what the surrounding context might be later in the year. We completely understand and don't want you as players to feel like you're voting based on changes we might end up making down the line, since it detracts from your ability to make an informed vote.

To sum all of the above up into a convenient tldr:

* Execution of 'big accuracy number' isn't easy to understand at a glance for players in terms of how it stacks up against other options.
* Competes too closely with the Avernic Defender, hurting its position in the game and thus ToB's loot table.
* Results in situations where other Slash weapons powercreep on the Scythe of Vitur, which also hurts ToB's loot table.
* Perhaps too strong when paired with the Dragon Dagger's Special Attack, has the potential to displace Dragon Claws.
* Results in significant imbalances in PvP, which is not at all our intent with this addition.
Taking all of the above into account, we made the decision to not move forward with the Bladed Moon at this time. This doesn't mean we're not open to exploring a healthier means of achieving that 'mid-game, Slash upgrade' with another piece of content in the future, as well as taking a look at Crush progression (since this is something else many of you have been talking about since we first released the blog). 

Perilous Moons still boasts three really cool armour sets that you can grab for yourself or to sell on for GP, and without giving too much away we'd like to say that the fights themselves are just ''fun''. We'll likely look to replace the Bladed Moon with an untradeable that ties directly in to quality-of-life when interacting with Varlamore or Perilous Moons specifically, something like a convenient teleport offering or some other buff to the content itself, but ultimately feel like the content itself is still in a really healthy spot and can't wait to see you all get stuck in when it releases later this year!

Before we sign off, we'd like to reiterate that for the questions surrounding the Glaive of Ralos and Echo Crystals, if those questions '''don't''' pass, then the pre-existing iterations will still be implemented, meaning the weaker Glaive of Ralos and Echo Crystals pairing with Guardian Boots, since they've already passed!

That's all we've got for you today, thanks for reading, make sure to get voting next week and we can't wait to see you in Varlamore - go and complete Children of the Sun already, and we'll know if you've spacebarred it...

